"90_winui_probe.mst — WG0, the first brick of the Windows-native Smalltalk
 UI (docs/win_gui_design.md §2.2 + §3 row WG0;
 docs/sprints/sprint_wg0_detail.md). Loaded ONLY via world/winui.list — the
 cocoaui.list mechanism (CG1's load_list) — so world.list stays
 byte-identical and the CLI, the web GUI and the base suite carry none of
 this. Nothing here is UI: WG0 proves HANDLES, not pixels.

 The architecture this answers to, in the author's own words:

     UI VM on the UI thread, messaging to a Smalltalk independent GUI
     layer. All in Smalltalk, with COM and API.

 So the whole call ladder below is Smalltalk. Rust owns the resolver, the
 trampolines and the GC; every decision about Win32 — which constant, which
 offset, which order — is made here, in a file a user can open in the
 Browser and edit while it runs.

 ── The ladder (D1), in the order it is proven ────────────────────────────
   1. GetSystemMetrics(SM_CXSCREEN)   int in, int out — first guest-visible
                                      user32 value
   2. MessageBeep(MB_OK)              BOOL semantics, audible side effect
   3. GetModuleHandleW(NULL)          the HINSTANCE every registration needs
   4. RegisterClassW(&wc)             a WNDCLASSW built in an Alien at
                                      winkb-QUERIED offsets
   5. CreateWindowExW(...)            hidden — no WS_VISIBLE
   6. IsWindow / DestroyWindow / IsWindow    the lifecycle round-trip

 ── Four facts this file exists to encode ────────────────────────────────

 1. NUMBERS COME FROM THE DATABASE (D2). Every constant is asked for by
    NAME at run time (`WinProbe constant: 'WS_OVERLAPPEDWINDOW'`), and every
    struct field's offset likewise (`WinProbe offsetOf:field:`). Nothing
    below transcribes a value from a header. NAMES are transcribed — the
    same status as the function names in the FFI pragmas — because a name
    is the question; the value is the answer, and the answer lives in
    winkb.

 2. THE HEAP MOVES; FOREIGN MEMORY MUST NOT. A direct `Alien new:` is
    ordinary GC-managed heap storage that a scavenge can relocate, so its
    address must NEVER cross the FFI (world/61a's NativeBuffer rule). Every
    byte Win32 will read — the WNDCLASSW, the class name, the window title
    — is bump-allocated out of one VirtualAlloc'd arena instead. `String >>
    asUtf16Alien` answers a direct Alien on purpose (it is a pure
    conversion, testable with no database and no Win32 at all) and
    `nativeUtf16:` is the copy that makes it callable.

 3. A 32-BIT RETURN'S UPPER HALF IS UNSPECIFIED (P5's Δ 1). AAPCS64 leaves
    x0's top 32 bits undefined for an `int`-returning callee, and Windows
    materialises them with w-register writes, i.e. ZERO-extended: an i32
    -1 arrives as 4294967295 through `ret: #g`. Every i32-returning binding
    here narrows through `i32:`, as world/tests/59_win_io_tests.mst's own
    header Δ requires.

 4. REGISTRATION IS NOT IDEMPOTENT, AND GetLastError CANNOT ADJUDICATE IT
    (pitfall 1, plus what measuring it taught). RegisterClassW answers 0
    the second time, and a suite re-runs in one process — but reading
    GetLastError to tell already-exists from a real failure FAILS under the
    JIT, because compilation and the W^X guard make Win32 calls between two
    guest sends and reset the thread's error (see `ensureClassRegistered:`).
    So the tolerance asks Win32 the real question instead —
    `GetClassInfoW`, is-this-class-usable — and `withClassRegisteredDo:`
    unregisters in an `ensure:` block so a failure mid-ladder cannot poison
    the next run. The class name is also unique per VM, because several VMs
    share one process's class namespace (see `probeClassName`).

 ── Deliberately NOT here ────────────────────────────────────────────────
 No message loop, no visible window, no WndProc other than DefWindowProcW's
 own address (WG1 owns the loop, WG2 owns the door). No DWM/Mica, no
 controls, no COM. And the window below is created on whatever thread the
 doit runs on, which is legitimate ONLY because it is hidden, pumps no
 messages and is destroyed immediately — WG1 moves window ownership to the
 hosted main-thread UI VM precisely because none of those three will hold
 once a window is real."

"WinRef is FORWARD-DECLARED here, with its instance variables, and reopened
 with its methods at the bottom of this file. The two classes name each
 other — WinProbe answers a WinRef from openHiddenWindow:, WinRef asks
 WinProbe whether its HWND is still alive — and a reference to a class not
 yet installed is a compile error in this dialect (the same forward-
 reference problem world/49_galaxigans.mst solves the same way, and the
 same one cocoaui.list's own comment records about load ORDER fixing what
 filename numbering does not)."
Object subclass: WinRef [ | handle kind | ]

"── WinProbe ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 I am the Windows FFI probe: the smallest thing that can prove guest
 Smalltalk reaches Win32 through winkb and the A64 trampolines. My class
 side carries the raw bindings (one FFI pragma each, fixed arity, exactly
 the signature windows_api.db records) and the ladder that uses them. Ask
 me `screenWidth`, `beep`, `moduleHandle`, or run the whole thing with
 `openHiddenWindow:` / `closeWindow:`. I am a PROBE, not a window system —
 WinShell (WG1) is what grows here."
Object subclass: WinProbe [
    <classVars: Arena ArenaNext ArenaLimit User32Handle DefWindowProcAddr
                Utf16Cache ProbeClassName ClassInfoScratch>

    "── winkb: the knowledge base, made guest-visible (WG0) ──────────────
     P5 wired winkb into the RESOLVER only: `lookup_function` feeds
     `resolve_ffi_symbol`, while the constants (97,402 rows) and struct
     layouts (66,708 rows) it also carries were reachable from Rust alone.
     Primitives 268-271 (src/runtime/primitives.rs, `prim_winkb_available`'s
     doc records the finding) expose the data half with NO policy: they
     answer what the database says, or nil. Every decision about the answer
     is made in the four methods below."

    WinProbe class >> primWinkbAvailable ^ <Boolean> [
        <primitive: 268>
        ^false
    ]
    WinProbe class >> primWinkbConstant: aName <String> ^ <Object> [
        <primitive: 269>
        ^nil
    ]
    WinProbe class >> primWinkbStructField: aStruct <String> field: aField <String> ^ <Object> [
        <primitive: 270>
        ^nil
    ]
    WinProbe class >> primWinkbStructSize: aStruct <String> ^ <Object> [
        <primitive: 271>
        ^nil
    ]

    WinProbe class >> winkbAvailable ^ <Boolean> [
        "Is windows_api.db on this machine? The database is a ~90 MB
         machine-local artifact, NOT a build input (winkb's founding
         contract: absence is not an error), so every caller of mine must be
         able to ask this and skip out loud. Answers false on macOS too —
         winkb is a Windows-only module — which is what lets this file LOAD
         everywhere and only RUN here."
        ^self primWinkbAvailable == true
    ]

    WinProbe class >> constant: aName <String> ^ <Integer> [
        "A named Win32 constant, from the database. NEVER transcribe one of
         these into Smalltalk source (D2): a hand-copied number is how a
         port drifts, and drift in a flag word does not fault — it produces
         a window with the wrong style and no error anywhere. The value
         arrives with C's own signedness (CW_USEDEFAULT is -2147483648, not
         2^63-ish), which is both what the header says and the only form
         that fits a SmallInt and therefore the only form the FFI can
         marshal."
        | v |
        v := self primWinkbConstant: aName.
        v isNil ifTrue: [
            ^self error: 'winkb: no constant named ' , aName printString ,
                (self winkbAvailable
                    ifTrue: [ ' in windows_api.db' ]
                    ifFalse: [ ' — and windows_api.db is not on this machine' ]) ].
        ^v
    ]

    WinProbe class >> offsetOf: aStruct <String> field: aField <String> ^ <Integer> [
        "A struct member's byte offset, 0-based as C counts it. This is what
         makes a Smalltalk-built WNDCLASSW correct by DERIVATION instead of
         by transcription: the guest asks where lpfnWndProc lives and writes
         there, so a padding rule this file never learned still comes out
         right."
        | v |
        v := self primWinkbStructField: aStruct field: aField.
        v isNil ifTrue: [
            ^self error: 'winkb: no field ' , aStruct , '.' , aField , ' recorded' ].
        ^v
    ]

    WinProbe class >> sizeOf: aStruct <String> ^ <Integer> [
        "The struct's total size — the allocation half of build-by-layout.
         Deriving it from the last member's offset would silently drop tail
         padding, and it comes from a DIFFERENT database column than the
         offsets do, which is what makes `size = lastOffset + lastWidth` a
         real cross-check rather than a tautology (the invariant
         world/tests/60_winui_probe_tests.mst asserts)."
        | v |
        v := self primWinkbStructSize: aStruct.
        v isNil ifTrue: [ ^self error: 'winkb: no struct named ' , aStruct , ' recorded' ].
        ^v
    ]

    "── the raw bindings ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
     One pragma each, fixed arity, and the arity/float-mask/return-class
     MUST match what windows_api.db records or P5's resolver refuses the
     call loudly before it happens (runtime::ffi::resolve_ffi_symbol's
     cross-check). Every parameter and return below is g-class: Win32's
     handles (HWND, HINSTANCE, HMODULE), its BOOLs and its ATOMs are all
     FP-free composites of 8 bytes or fewer, which the MS-ARM64 classifier
     models as one GPR (winkb's D2 table)."

    WinProbe class >> ffiVirtualAllocAddr: a <Integer> size: s <Integer> type: t <Integer> protect: p <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #VirtualAlloc ret: #g args: #(g g g g)>
    ]
    WinProbe class >> ffiVirtualFree: a <Integer> size: s <Integer> type: t <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #VirtualFree ret: #g args: #(g g g)>
    ]
    WinProbe class >> ffiGetSystemMetrics: index <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetSystemMetrics ret: #g args: #(g)>
    ]
    WinProbe class >> ffiMessageBeep: style <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #MessageBeep ret: #g args: #(g)>
    ]
    WinProbe class >> ffiGetModuleHandleW: namePtr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetModuleHandleW ret: #g args: #(g)>
    ]
    "GetProcAddress is how a Smalltalk-side caller learns a function's
     ADDRESS rather than calling it: the FFI pragma resolves by NAME and
     hands the address to the trampoline, never to the guest, and WG0 needs
     DefWindowProcW's address as DATA (it goes in a struct field). Its
     second argument is a PSTR — ANSI, not wide — one of the few places in
     this file that is not UTF-16."
    WinProbe class >> ffiGetProcAddressIn: hModule <Integer> named: namePtr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetProcAddress ret: #g args: #(g g)>
    ]
    WinProbe class >> ffiRegisterClassW: wndClassPtr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #RegisterClassW ret: #g args: #(g)>
    ]
    WinProbe class >> ffiUnregisterClassW: namePtr <Integer> instance: hInstance <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #UnregisterClassW ret: #g args: #(g g)>
    ]
    "GetClassInfoW answers whether a class name is registered for an
     HINSTANCE — 'is this class USABLE', which is the question
     ensureClassRegistered: actually needs and the one GetLastError turned
     out not to be able to answer here (see that method's Δ)."
    WinProbe class >> ffiGetClassInfoW: hInstance <Integer> name: namePtr <Integer> into: wcPtr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetClassInfoW ret: #g args: #(g g g)>
    ]
    "Twelve g-class arguments: the first eight ride x0-x7 and the last four
     SPILL TO THE STACK at [sp, #0..24] (codecache::ffi_stubs' one uniform
     16-word shape). MS ARM64 and Apple AAPCS64 agree on 8-byte stack slots
     for 8-byte values, which is why the emitters written for the Mac carry
     this call unchanged — but WG0 is the first caller on EITHER platform to
     push more than eight integers through a Win32 entry point, so if the
     spill were wrong this is where it would show."
    WinProbe class >> ffiCreateWindowExStyle: exStyle <Integer> class: classPtr <Integer> title: titlePtr <Integer> style: style <Integer> x: x <Integer> y: y <Integer> width: w <Integer> height: h <Integer> parent: parent <Integer> menu: menu <Integer> instance: hInstance <Integer> param: lpParam <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #CreateWindowExW ret: #g args: #(g g g g g g g g g g g g)>
    ]
    WinProbe class >> ffiIsWindow: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #IsWindow ret: #g args: #(g)>
    ]
    WinProbe class >> ffiDestroyWindow: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #DestroyWindow ret: #g args: #(g)>
    ]
    "GetLastError has a discipline attached (winkb's module doc, measured in
     P5): a binding's FIRST call runs resolution, and resolution itself
     performs Win32 calls that reset the thread's last-error — so a cold
     GetLastError reads 0 after a genuine failure. Warm it (see
     `warmErrorBinding`), then read it as the IMMEDIATELY next FFI call."
    WinProbe class >> ffiGetLastError ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetLastError ret: #g args: #()>
    ]

    "── narrowing ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinProbe class >> i32: v <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "Narrow a C `int` read raw out of x0. AAPCS64 leaves the upper half
         UNSPECIFIED for a 32-bit return and Windows zero-extends it, so -1
         arrives as 4294967295 — the width is simply not expressible in the
         g/f token vocabulary and the binding's author owns it (P5's Δ 1,
         world/tests/59_win_io_tests.mst)."
        ^v >= 2147483648 ifTrue: [ v - 4294967296 ] ifFalse: [ v ]
    ]

    WinProbe class >> asBool: v <Integer> ^ <Boolean> [
        "Win32 BOOL: any nonzero is true. Never compare against 1 — several
         user32 entries answer other nonzero values on success."
        ^(self i32: v) ~= 0
    ]

    "── the arena: GC-stable memory for anything Win32 will read ─────────
     One VirtualAlloc'd region, bump-allocated, never freed for the life of
     the VM. Sixty-four kilobytes is far more than the probe needs; the
     point is not capacity but STABILITY — a scavenge relocating a string
     Win32 still holds a pointer to would be a use-after-move with no fault
     at the point of error, the exact bug class this port keeps refusing.
     `freeArena` exists for symmetry and for a test that wants a clean
     slate; nothing calls it in the normal flow."

    WinProbe class >> arenaBytes ^ <Integer> [ ^65536 ]

    WinProbe class >> arenaBase ^ <Integer> [
        | addr |
        Arena isNil ifTrue: [
            addr := self
                ffiVirtualAllocAddr: 0
                size: self arenaBytes
                type: ((self constant: 'MEM_COMMIT') bitOr: (self constant: 'MEM_RESERVE'))
                protect: (self constant: 'PAGE_READWRITE').
            (addr isNil or: [ addr = 0 ]) ifTrue: [
                ^self error: 'WinProbe: VirtualAlloc of the ' , self arenaBytes printString ,
                    '-byte probe arena failed (GetLastError ' ,
                    self ffiGetLastError printString , ')' ].
            Arena := addr.
            ArenaNext := 0.
            ArenaLimit := self arenaBytes ].
        ^Arena
    ]

    WinProbe class >> arenaAlloc: nBytes <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "Answer the ADDRESS of nBytes of zeroed, GC-stable, 8-aligned
         storage. VirtualAlloc zero-fills its pages, and nothing here ever
         re-uses a block, so the zeroing holds — which matters for a struct
         whose unset members (hIcon, hCursor, hbrBackground, lpszMenuName)
         must be NULL."
        | base off end |
        base := self arenaBase.
        off := ArenaNext.
        end := off + nBytes.
        end > ArenaLimit ifTrue: [
            ^self error: 'WinProbe: probe arena exhausted (' , ArenaLimit printString ,
                ' bytes); WG1 will want a real allocator here' ].
        ArenaNext := end + ((8 - (end \\ 8)) \\ 8).
        ^base + off
    ]

    WinProbe class >> arenaUsed ^ <Integer> [ ^ArenaNext isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ ArenaNext ] ]

    WinProbe class >> freeArena [
        "Release the arena and forget every address derived from it. Any
         Alien still wrapping one of those addresses is now dangling — this
         is a test/teardown verb, not a general-purpose free."
        Arena isNil ifFalse: [
            self ffiVirtualFree: Arena size: 0 type: (self constant: 'MEM_RELEASE').
            Arena := nil. ArenaNext := nil. ArenaLimit := nil.
            User32Handle := nil. DefWindowProcAddr := nil.
            Utf16Cache := nil. ProbeClassName := nil. ClassInfoScratch := nil ].
        ^self
    ]

    "── strings: Win32 is UTF-16 (D3) ────────────────────────────────────
     The ...W entries are the design's own Windows-materials stance applied
     to the character set; the ...A entries are a shortcut this port does
     not take. `String >> asUtf16Alien` (below, on String itself) does the
     encoding; these two copy the result somewhere Win32 may safely read
     it, and answer the ADDRESS, because Alien publishes no address
     accessor to the guest at all."

    WinProbe class >> nativeUtf16: aString <String> ^ <Integer> [
        "MEMOISED by string value, and that is not an optimisation: the
         arena is bump-allocated and never reclaims, so re-encoding the same
         class name on every one of twenty ladders would be a slow leak with
         a cliff at the end of the region. The cache is safe because these
         strings are literals; a caller that MUTATED one it had already
         passed here would get the old bytes, which is why nothing does."
        | src n addr dst hit |
        Utf16Cache isNil ifTrue: [ Utf16Cache := Dictionary new ].
        hit := Utf16Cache at: aString ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        hit isNil ifFalse: [ ^hit ].
        src := aString asUtf16Alien.
        n := src size.
        addr := self arenaAlloc: n.
        dst := Alien forAddress: addr size: n.
        1 to: n do: [ :i | dst byteAt: i put: (src byteAt: i) ].
        Utf16Cache at: aString put: addr.
        ^addr
    ]

    WinProbe class >> nativeAscii: aString <String> ^ <Integer> [
        "The narrow twin, needed exactly once: GetProcAddress takes a PSTR.
         A symbol name is ASCII by definition, so no encoding question
         arises — and this method deliberately does not pretend to be a
         general Latin-1 path."
        | n addr dst |
        n := aString size + 1.
        addr := self arenaAlloc: n.
        dst := Alien forAddress: addr size: n.
        1 to: aString size do: [ :i | dst byteAt: i put: (aString at: i) asInteger ].
        dst byteAt: n put: 0.
        ^addr
    ]

    "── ladder step 1-3 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinProbe class >> screenWidth ^ <Integer> [
        "GetSystemMetrics(SM_CXSCREEN) — the first guest-visible user32
         value in this port's history. Int in, int out, zero risk: if this
         answers a plausible width, the whole chain (pragma → winkb lookup →
         cross-check → GetProcAddress → A64 trampoline → user32) is alive."
        ^self i32: (self ffiGetSystemMetrics: (self constant: 'SM_CXSCREEN'))
    ]

    WinProbe class >> screenHeight ^ <Integer> [
        ^self i32: (self ffiGetSystemMetrics: (self constant: 'SM_CYSCREEN'))
    ]

    WinProbe class >> beep ^ <Boolean> [
        "MessageBeep(MB_OK) — BOOL semantics with an audible side effect
         (which nothing asserts: a test that needed to hear something would
         not be headless)."
        ^self asBool: (self ffiMessageBeep: (self constant: 'MB_OK'))
    ]

    WinProbe class >> moduleHandle ^ <Integer> [
        "GetModuleHandleW(NULL) — this process's own HINSTANCE, the one
         every window class registers against. NULL is a genuine null
         POINTER argument (0), not a missing one."
        ^self ffiGetModuleHandleW: 0
    ]

    WinProbe class >> user32Handle ^ <Integer> [
        | h |
        User32Handle isNil ifTrue: [
            h := self ffiGetModuleHandleW: (self nativeUtf16: 'user32.dll').
            h = 0 ifTrue: [ ^self error: 'WinProbe: user32.dll is not loaded in this process' ].
            User32Handle := h ].
        ^User32Handle
    ]

    WinProbe class >> defWindowProcAddress ^ <Integer> [
        "DefWindowProcW's own address, for the lpfnWndProc member. WG0 hands
         Win32 the SYSTEM's default handler: there is no door yet, no
         dispatch into Smalltalk, and no Rust trampoline — WG2 owns that,
         and win_gui_design.md §2.3 records why it will be structurally
         simpler than Cocoa's C6 (one uniform four-word signature, no
         runtime class synthesis)."
        | a |
        DefWindowProcAddr isNil ifTrue: [
            a := self ffiGetProcAddressIn: self user32Handle
                     named: (self nativeAscii: 'DefWindowProcW').
            (a isNil or: [ a = 0 ]) ifTrue: [
                ^self error: 'WinProbe: GetProcAddress(user32, DefWindowProcW) answered NULL' ].
            DefWindowProcAddr := a ].
        ^DefWindowProcAddr
    ]

    "── ladder step 4: the WNDCLASSW, by layout ──────────────────────────"

    WinProbe class >> wndclassFieldNames ^ <Array> [
        "The MEMBER NAMES, in declaration order — identifiers, exactly like
         the #RegisterClassW in the pragma above, and the only part of the
         layout this file states. Every offset and the total size are asked
         of winkb (`wndclassOffsets`, `sizeOf:`).

         Δ (WG0, measured): sprint_wg0_detail.md D1 step 4 calls WNDCLASSW
         'a 16-entry struct'. windows_api.db records TEN members, 72 bytes
         (WNDCLASSEXW — which WG0 does not use — has twelve, 80 bytes).
         Pinned in runtime::winkb's real-DB test."
        ^#('style' 'lpfnWndProc' 'cbClsExtra' 'cbWndExtra' 'hInstance'
           'hIcon' 'hCursor' 'hbrBackground' 'lpszMenuName' 'lpszClassName')
    ]

    WinProbe class >> wndclassOffsets ^ <Dictionary> [
        "Every member's offset, queried by name. `offsetOf:field:` raises on
         a name the database does not carry, so a missing answer is a named
         error and never a silent 0 written over the style word."
        | d |
        d := Dictionary new.
        self wndclassFieldNames do: [ :f |
            d at: f put: (self offsetOf: 'WNDCLASSW' field: f) ].
        ^d
    ]

    WinProbe class >> buildWindowClassNamed: aName <String> ^ <Integer> [
        "Build a WNDCLASSW in GC-stable memory and answer its address.
         Members are written THROUGH the queried offsets — the point of the
         exercise — with `signedLongAt:put:` (an 8-byte write at a 1-based
         byte index; Alien's own accessor family is 1-, 8- and double-wide,
         so the two i32 members ride the same 8-byte write as their
         neighbours' padding, which is correct here only because the arena
         is zeroed and both are 0).

         What is deliberately left NULL: hIcon, hCursor, hbrBackground and
         lpszMenuName. A cursor-less, brush-less class is fine for a window
         that is never shown and never painted; WG1 fills them."
        | off addr wc |
        off := self wndclassOffsets.
        addr := self arenaAlloc: (self sizeOf: 'WNDCLASSW').
        wc := Alien forAddress: addr size: (self sizeOf: 'WNDCLASSW').
        wc signedLongAt: (off at: 'style') + 1
           put: ((self constant: 'CS_HREDRAW') bitOr: (self constant: 'CS_VREDRAW')).
        wc signedLongAt: (off at: 'lpfnWndProc') + 1 put: self defWindowProcAddress.
        wc signedLongAt: (off at: 'hInstance') + 1 put: self moduleHandle.
        wc signedLongAt: (off at: 'lpszClassName') + 1 put: (self nativeUtf16: aName).
        ^addr
    ]

    WinProbe class >> warmErrorBinding [
        "One throwaway GetLastError so the binding is RESOLVED. P5 measured
         this: the first call through any binding runs resolution, and
         resolution performs Win32 calls (the sqlite open,
         GetModuleHandle/LoadLibrary/GetProcAddress) that reset the thread's
         last-error — so `fail(); GetLastError()` reads 0 on a cold
         binding."
        self ffiGetLastError.
        ^self
    ]

    WinProbe class >> classExists: aName <String> ^ <Boolean> [
        "Is aName registered for this process's HINSTANCE? GetClassInfoW
         fills a WNDCLASSW it is given and answers a BOOL; the buffer's
         contents are of no interest here, only the answer — so ONE scratch
         buffer serves every call (the arena never reclaims, and this runs
         on every re-registration)."
        ClassInfoScratch isNil ifTrue: [
            ClassInfoScratch := self arenaAlloc: (self sizeOf: 'WNDCLASSW') ].
        ^self asBool: (self
            ffiGetClassInfoW: self moduleHandle
            name: (self nativeUtf16: aName)
            into: ClassInfoScratch)
    ]

    WinProbe class >> ensureClassRegistered: aName <String> ^ <Boolean> [
        "RegisterClassW, tolerating the second time (pitfall 1). A window
         class is per-process state that outlives a doit, and a test suite
         re-runs in one process — a probe that passes once and fails forever
         after is a trap for WG1.

         Δ (WG0, MEASURED the hard way): the obvious implementation reads
         GetLastError after a zero atom and tolerates
         ERROR_CLASS_ALREADY_EXISTS. It works in isolation and FAILS under
         the JIT: at a low compile threshold this call site read 203
         (ERROR_ENVVAR_NOT_FOUND) — a leftover from somebody else's Win32
         call entirely. P5's GetLastError discipline says nothing between
         two cached guest FFI sends performs a Win32 call, and that is true
         of marshalling but NOT of the VM underneath it: compilation, the
         W^X guard's VirtualProtect flips and code-cache VirtualAlloc all
         run between guest sends and all reset the thread's last error.
         There is no guest-side way to close that window.

         So the tolerance is decided by the question the sprint doc itself
         poses — 'assert the class is usable either way' — asked of Win32
         directly with GetClassInfoW. The error code is still read, but only
         to REPORT with, and the report says it may be stale. Any Win32
         logic that BRANCHES on GetLastError under this VM is fragile in
         exactly this way; WG1 and WG2 should treat it as a diagnostic, not
         a control input."
        | atom |
        self warmErrorBinding.
        atom := self ffiRegisterClassW: (self buildWindowClassNamed: aName).
        atom ~= 0 ifTrue: [ ^true ].
        (self classExists: aName) ifTrue: [ ^true ].
        ^self error: 'WinProbe: RegisterClassW(' , aName ,
            ') answered 0 and the class is not registered; GetLastError = ' ,
            self ffiGetLastError printString ,
            ' (which the JIT may have clobbered — see ensureClassRegistered:)'
    ]

    WinProbe class >> unregisterClass: aName <String> ^ <Boolean> [
        ^self asBool: (self ffiUnregisterClassW: (self nativeUtf16: aName)
                            instance: self moduleHandle)
    ]

    "── ladder steps 5-6: create hidden, prove alive, destroy ────────────"

    WinProbe class >> probeClassName ^ <String> [
        "Unique per VM, because a window class is registered against the
         PROCESS and two VMs share one.

         Δ (WG0, MEASURED): the suite runs several VMs in one process, in
         parallel test threads, and each ran the same ladder — so one VM's
         UnregisterClassW could pull the class out from under another VM's
         CreateWindowExW, and the failures looked like flakes. The arena's
         base address is unique per live VM in a process (nothing ever
         frees it), so it is exactly the discriminator needed and it costs
         no new state. Within ONE VM the name is stable, which is what
         keeps the re-registration pitfall genuinely exercised.

         WG1 inherits the general form of this: window classes, atoms, the
         message queue and the last-error value are all PER-PROCESS or
         per-thread state that the two-VM split (win_gui_design.md §2.2)
         has to reason about explicitly."
        ProbeClassName isNil ifTrue: [
            ProbeClassName := 'MacvmWG0Probe_' , (self arenaBase printString: 16) ].
        ^ProbeClassName
    ]

    WinProbe class >> openHiddenWindow: aTitle <String> ^ <WinRef> [
        "CreateWindowExW with NO WS_VISIBLE. WG0 proves handles, not pixels:
         a visible window with no message loop behind it is a hung white
         rectangle, and the loop belongs to WG1's main-thread UI VM.
         CW_USEDEFAULT arrives from the database as -2147483648 — C's own
         signed reading — and marshals to the identical register bits the
         header's 0x80000000 would."
        | hwnd |
        self ensureClassRegistered: self probeClassName.
        hwnd := self
            ffiCreateWindowExStyle: 0
            class: (self nativeUtf16: self probeClassName)
            title: (self nativeUtf16: aTitle)
            style: (self constant: 'WS_OVERLAPPEDWINDOW')
            x: (self constant: 'CW_USEDEFAULT')
            y: (self constant: 'CW_USEDEFAULT')
            width: 320
            height: 200
            parent: 0
            menu: 0
            instance: self moduleHandle
            param: 0.
        hwnd = 0 ifTrue: [
            ^self error: 'WinProbe: CreateWindowExW failed, GetLastError = ' ,
                self ffiGetLastError printString ].
        ^WinRef onHandle: hwnd
    ]

    WinProbe class >> isWindow: aWinRef <WinRef> ^ <Boolean> [
        ^self asBool: (self ffiIsWindow: aWinRef handle)
    ]

    WinProbe class >> destroyWindow: aWinRef <WinRef> ^ <Boolean> [
        ^self asBool: (self ffiDestroyWindow: aWinRef handle)
    ]

    WinProbe class >> withClassRegisteredDo: aBlock [
        "Run aBlock with the probe class registered, and unregister on the
         way out WHATEVER happens (tests_wg0.md gate item 3). `ensure:`
         because an intermediate failure must not leave the class behind:
         the next run would then take the already-exists path and never
         exercise a real registration again."
        self ensureClassRegistered: self probeClassName.
        ^[ aBlock value ] ensure: [ self unregisterClass: self probeClassName ]
    ]

    WinProbe class >> runLadder ^ <Dictionary> [
        "The whole of D1 in one doit, answering what each rung returned,
         keyed by rung — the shape a Workspace (or a sprint report) wants.
         Cleans up after itself, so it is safe to run repeatedly in one
         process; running it twenty times in a row is a test."
        | out ref |
        out := Dictionary new.
        out at: 'screenWidth' put: self screenWidth.
        out at: 'beep' put: self beep.
        out at: 'hInstance' put: self moduleHandle.
        out at: 'defWindowProc' put: self defWindowProcAddress.
        [ ref := self openHiddenWindow: 'MACVM WG0 probe'.
          out at: 'hwnd' put: ref handle.
          out at: 'isWindowBefore' put: (self isWindow: ref).
          out at: 'destroyed' put: (self destroyWindow: ref).
          out at: 'isWindowAfter' put: (self isWindow: ref) ]
            ensure: [ self unregisterClass: self probeClassName ].
        ^out
    ]

    WinProbe class >> report [
        "Human-readable ladder, for a Workspace or a CLI doit."
        Transcript showCr: 'WG0 ladder (winkb ' ,
            (self winkbAvailable ifTrue: [ 'present' ] ifFalse: [ 'ABSENT' ]) , '):'.
        self winkbAvailable ifFalse: [
            Transcript showCr: '  SKIP — windows_api.db is not on this machine; every'.
            Transcript showCr: '  constant and struct offset below comes from it (D2).'.
            ^self ].
        Transcript showCr: '  1 GetSystemMetrics(SM_CXSCREEN) = ' , self screenWidth printString.
        Transcript showCr: '  2 MessageBeep(MB_OK) = ' , self beep printString.
        Transcript showCr: '  3 GetModuleHandleW(NULL) = 16r' , (self moduleHandle printString: 16).
        Transcript showCr: '    DefWindowProcW = 16r' , (self defWindowProcAddress printString: 16).
        Transcript showCr: '  4 WNDCLASSW = ' , (self sizeOf: 'WNDCLASSW') printString ,
            ' bytes, ' , self wndclassFieldNames size printString , ' members, lpfnWndProc@' ,
            (self offsetOf: 'WNDCLASSW' field: 'lpfnWndProc') printString.
        [ | ref |
          ref := self openHiddenWindow: 'MACVM WG0 probe'.
          Transcript showCr: '  5 CreateWindowExW (hidden) = ' , ref printString.
          Transcript showCr: '  6 IsWindow = ' , (self isWindow: ref) printString.
          Transcript showCr: '    DestroyWindow = ' , (self destroyWindow: ref) printString.
          Transcript showCr: '    IsWindow = ' , (self isWindow: ref) printString ]
            ensure: [ self unregisterClass: self probeClassName ].
        ^self
    ]
]

"── WinRef ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 I wrap one Windows handle so a guest program never passes a bare integer
 around. This is a SKETCH: WG0 needs the probe's HWND to have an honest
 home, and nothing more. The full protocol — message sending, the
 doesNotUnderstand: path, lifetime, the COM sibling (win_gui_design.md
 §2.4's ComRef) — belongs to WG2 and after, and is deliberately absent
 rather than half-built.

 What I do NOT do, on purpose:
   * I do not own my handle. DestroyWindow is the caller's call; there are
     no finalizers in this VM (weak refs are a later slice), so an owning
     WinRef would be a promise the runtime cannot keep.
   * I do not cache liveness. `isAlive` asks Win32 every time, because a
     handle can be destroyed by anyone — including the system — and a
     cached answer is a lie with a timestamp.
   * I am not an ObjcRef twin. ObjcRef wraps a retained `id` with real
     lifetime semantics; an HWND is a process-wide token, not a refcount."
Object subclass: WinRef [

    WinRef class >> onHandle: anInteger <Integer> ^ <WinRef> [
        ^self onHandle: anInteger kind: #HWND
    ]
    WinRef class >> onHandle: anInteger <Integer> kind: aSymbol <Symbol> ^ <WinRef> [
        ^self basicNew setHandle: anInteger kind: aSymbol
    ]
    setHandle: anInteger <Integer> kind: aSymbol <Symbol> [
        handle := anInteger.
        kind := aSymbol.
        ^self
    ]

    handle ^ <Integer> [ ^handle ]
    kind ^ <Symbol> [ ^kind ]
    isNull ^ <Boolean> [ ^handle isNil or: [ handle = 0 ] ]

    isAlive ^ <Boolean> [
        "Only an HWND can answer this — IsWindow is a window question, and
         answering false for a live HANDLE of some other kind would be a
         lie. Kinds I cannot check say so by raising rather than guessing."
        kind == #HWND ifFalse: [
            ^self error: 'WinRef: liveness is only checkable for an HWND, not ' , kind printString ].
        self isNull ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        ^WinProbe isWindow: self
    ]

    printOn: aStream <WriteStream> [
        "Honest: the kind, the handle in hex, and — for an HWND — whether
         Win32 still knows it. A destroyed window prints `dead`, which is
         the whole reason this class exists rather than a bare integer."
        aStream nextPutAll: 'a WinRef('.
        aStream nextPutAll: kind printString.
        aStream nextPutAll: ' 16r'.
        aStream nextPutAll: (handle isNil ifTrue: [ 'nil' ] ifFalse: [ handle printString: 16 ]).
        kind == #HWND ifTrue: [
            aStream nextPutAll: (self isAlive ifTrue: [ ' live' ] ifFalse: [ ' dead' ]) ].
        aStream nextPutAll: ')'
    ]

    = anObject ^ <Boolean> [
        ^(anObject isKindOf: WinRef) and: [ anObject handle = handle and: [ anObject kind == kind ] ]
    ]
    hash ^ <Integer> [ ^handle hash ]
]

"── String: UTF-16, once and for all (D3) ────────────────────────────────
 Win32's ...W entries take UTF-16LE, NUL-terminated. This is the encoder,
 written here, tested here (world/tests/60_winui_probe_tests.mst) and
 reused by every WG sprint after. It is pure Smalltalk: no database, no
 Win32, no platform check — so it works, and is testable, on macOS too."
ArrayedCollection subclass: String [

    utf16CodeUnits ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        "My Unicode scalars as UTF-16 code units, surrogate pairs included.
         Built on world/83's `codePoints` (which decodes me as UTF-8 — a
         String here is a BYTE vector, and pretending otherwise is what a
         text layer is for), so a malformed byte sequence raises rather than
         silently producing mojibake in a window title."
        | out v points |
        self isValidUtf8 ifFalse: [
            ^self error: 'asUtf16: the receiver is not valid UTF-8, so it has no code points' ].
        points := self codePoints.
        out := OrderedCollection new.
        points do: [ :cp |
            cp <= 16rFFFF
                ifTrue: [ out add: cp ]
                ifFalse: [
                    v := cp - 16r10000.
                    out add: 16rD800 + (v bitShift: -10).
                    out add: 16rDC00 + (v bitAnd: 16r3FF) ] ].
        ^out
    ]

    utf16ByteSize ^ <Integer> [
        "Bytes an `asUtf16Alien` of me occupies — code units, two bytes
         each, plus the two-byte NUL terminator."
        ^(self utf16CodeUnits size * 2) + 2
    ]

    asUtf16Alien ^ <Alien> [
        "Me as NUL-terminated UTF-16LE bytes in a DIRECT Alien.

         Direct — heap-backed — on purpose: this is a conversion, and its
         result is an ordinary movable object that GC may relocate at any
         time. Its address must therefore NEVER cross the FFI (world/61a's
         NativeBuffer rule); `WinProbe nativeUtf16:` copies these bytes into
         GC-stable arena memory and answers the address that may."
        | units a i |
        units := self utf16CodeUnits.
        a := Alien new: (units size * 2) + 2.
        i := 1.
        units do: [ :u |
            a byteAt: i put: (u bitAnd: 255).
            a byteAt: i + 1 put: ((u bitShift: -8) bitAnd: 255).
            i := i + 2 ].
        a byteAt: i put: 0.
        a byteAt: i + 1 put: 0.
        ^a
    ]
]
